// One RFC, one algorithm — where the package has three of each.
//
// RFC 4648 defines base16, base32 and base64 as the same construction at three widths: take the
// input as a bit stream, cut it into `bits`-wide digits, pad the last group. `packages/codec` writes
// that three times, 365 lines, and the three copies differ in the alphabet, the group size and
// nothing else.
//
// This is the whole package.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec } from "core";
import { Alphabet, Padding } from "./alphabet.wac";
import { CodecFault, NotADigit, ShortGroup, BadPadding, ImpossibleLength, NonZeroTail }
  from "./fault.wac";

/**
 * Bytes to text — and it answers a **`string`**, which the shipped one does not.
 *
 * No `Result`: every byte sequence has an encoding, which is the difference between this and
 * `decode` and is why the shipped `encode` answers `u8[]` and the shipped `decode` answers `u8[]?`.
 * Keeping *that* asymmetry is the point — a codec where one direction can fail and the other cannot
 * should say so in the two signatures.
 *
 * ## Why `string` out and `Bytes` in, which is not symmetry and is what the callers want
 *
 * Base64 output is ASCII by construction; there is no byte sequence `encode` can produce that is not
 * text. The shipped `encode` answers `u8[]` anyway, and the cost of that is measured rather than
 * argued — `issues/system/0339a`:
 *
 * `packages/tor/src/directory.wac` **hand-writes a whole second base64 encoder**, twenty lines with
 * its own alphabet literal, in a file that imports `packages/codec`'s *decoder* at line 28. Not
 * legacy: the codec and `string.fromBytes` both landed 2026-07-31 and that file was written
 * 2026-08-03, in one commit, with `string.fromBytes` used inside the hand-rolled version's own last
 * line. The whole function is `string.fromBytes(encode(bytes, ALPHABET_STANDARD(), false))`.
 *
 * The plausible cause is this signature. All six call sites want a `string` — a map key,
 * `byDigest.getOr(base64NoPad(sha256(body)), -1)`, and a URL path segment, `hsdir.wac:279`.
 *
 * The input side goes the other way and by the same evidence: `packages/tor/src/hsdesc.wac` and
 * `hsintro.wac` decode a block sliced out of a document, which is bytes, and only
 * `onionaddr.wac` starts from a string. **Two callers hold bytes going in and every caller wants a
 * string coming out**, so the asymmetric pair is the one the callers ask for, and symmetry here
 * would be a preference imposed on both.
 */
export string encode(Bytes data, Alphabet a, Padding pad) { … }

/**
 * Text to bytes, or the first reason it is not text this alphabet produced.
 *
 * **Strict, on all five counts, which is the shipped package's decision and its best one.** The
 * README's argument for the fifth is the one worth carrying over verbatim: without the unused-bits
 * check, *"`QQ==`, `QR==`, `QS==` and thirteen more all decode to `A` — so a signature over the text
 * says nothing about the bytes, and two systems can disagree about whether two messages are the
 * same. It costs one comparison."*
 *
 * What changes is that a caller can now tell the five apart, which matters most for exactly that
 * one: a `NonZeroTail` means somebody produced this text with a lenient encoder, and a `NotADigit`
 * means it is not base64 at all. The shipped `null` conflates *this is malformed* with *this is not
 * even the right format*, and those lead to different next steps.
 */
export Result<Bytes, CodecFault> decode(Bytes text, Alphabet a) { … }

/**
 * Whitespace is not skipped, and this is how a caller who wants it says so.
 *
 * The shipped README: *"'Ignore whitespace' is a decision the caller should make explicitly rather
 * than one a decoder makes for everybody, and a caller who wants it can strip before calling."*
 * Right, and then no caller has anywhere to get the stripping from, so each writes it. One function,
 * exported, so the decision stays the caller's and the loop is written once.
 */
export Bytes withoutSpace(Bytes text) { … }

// ## 1. Generalising the three replaced a table with a formula, and that is a real loss
//
// `packages/codec/src/base32.wac` has `PAD_FOR` and `DIGITS_FOR` indexed by the number of leftover
// bytes, and says why:
//
// > Five bytes become eight digits, so a short final group can be 1, 2, 3 or 4 bytes and pads to
// > 6, 4, 3 or 1 `=` respectively. Those numbers are not a pattern anyone remembers, so they are a
// > table rather than arithmetic.
//
// **RFC 4648 §6 states those four numbers in a table.** A reader checks the code against the RFC by
// reading four lines beside four lines. `Alphabet.groupDigits()` computes them, and a formula agrees
// with the RFC or does not, all at once — which is fine for a test suite and not the same thing for
// a reviewer. The shipped decision optimises for the check a person can perform; this one optimises
// for there being one implementation instead of three.
//
// Both are right and they are not reconcilable by choosing a nicer spelling. It is worth
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` because the trade-off recurs — every *"a table rather than arithmetic"*
// in this repository is the same bet — and because the deciding fact is which document the code is
// checked against, which nothing in a signature can carry.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`Alphabet.digit(v)` takes an `i32` in `0 .. 2^bits` and nothing says so.** The bound depends on
// the receiver, so it is not a type any parameter could have. The shipped code has the same hole and
// hides it better, because a per-base function has a per-base range that a reader can see. This is
// the cost of the generalisation showing up in a second place, and it is
// *where does a refinement of an integer live?* again.
//
// **`Padding` means nothing at base16 and the type cannot say it.** `Padded` and `Unpadded` produce
// identical output for `Base16Lower`, because a byte is exactly two digits. Nothing rejects the
// argument, nothing warns, and a reader has to know. A per-base signature — which is what the
// shipped package has — gets this right by construction and pays for it three times over.
//
// **The alphabet is a closed set of six and the RFC has five sections.** `Base16Lower` and
// `Base16Upper` are one section, §8, which defines base16 as uppercase; the lowercase variant exists
// because `hex.wac`'s `encode` is lowercase and its `encodeUpper` is the RFC's. So the enum encodes
// *what the package does* rather than *what the RFC says*, in the one place the two disagree, and
// naming them for the RFC would have hidden a real behaviour.
